<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelswith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 888</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelswith was the daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelwulf-of-wessex">Æthelwulf of Wessex</a> and sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>. In 853 she married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgred-of-mercia">Burgred of Mercia</a>, sealing the alliance between the two kingdoms. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> drove Burgred into exile in 874 she went with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and she died at Pavia in 888 while on pilgrimage. A gold finger ring bearing her name survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelswith.jpg" alt="Æthelswith" width="170" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%86thelswith,_Queen_of_Mercia,_in_British_Library_Cotton_MS_Claudius_B_VI.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
